<h2>A parked e-rickshaw, and then a mob</h2>
<p>The incident began with the most ordinary of urban frictions. On the midnight intervening 29 and 30 June 2026, the complainant Varun and his friend Arjun, on a motorcycle heading towards Ghonda Chowk, found an e-rickshaw parked in the middle of the road and asked the driver to move it. According to the FIR, the driver began abusing them and made a phone call; within minutes, around twenty persons — the applicant among them — had arrived and set upon the two with fists, kicks and a chair. Both victims were taken to hospital by PCR; Varun&rsquo;s nasal bone was fractured, an injury his MLC recorded as grievous. The entire episode was captured on CCTV.</p>

<h2>The rival submissions</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>For the applicant</h3><p>Nothing incriminating was recovered from him; the investigation against co-accused was complete; he was a mere bystander without common intention; and the injuries were simple.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>For the State</h3><p>The CCTV footage contradicts the bystander claim; the applicant failed to join the investigation despite three notices; his custodial interrogation is needed to identify the remaining assailants; and the MLC records a grievous injury.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The Court watched the footage</h2>
<p>What distinguishes the order is its directness with the evidence. The CCTV footage was played in court, and the judge recorded what it showed: fifteen to twenty assailants ganging up on two persons. On that viewing, the Court held that &ldquo;The CCTV footage does not support the version of learned counsel for accused/applicant that he was merely a bystander.&rdquo; The claim that the investigation was over also failed — the investigating officer clarified that it stood complete only as regards the two children in conflict with law, not the applicant, whose role in identifying the remaining members of the mob remained under investigation.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The signalling concern:</strong> emphasising that this was not a regular bail application after arrest but a plea for pre-arrest protection, the Court reasoned that granting anticipatory bail in such circumstances would &ldquo;send completely wrong signals to the society, encouraging such cases of road rage.&rdquo;</p>
</div>

<h2>Anticipatory bail is not a parallel trial</h2>
<p>The decision illustrates three working principles of anticipatory bail practice in Delhi. First, the stage matters: arguments that might succeed at regular bail — simple injuries, completed investigation, limited role — carry less weight when the police have not yet had the opportunity of custodial interrogation and the applicant has avoided notices under Section 35 BNSS. Second, objective evidence is increasingly decisive at the pre-arrest stage; where CCTV exists, courts will view it, and a defence narrative inconsistent with the footage can collapse in minutes. Third, the nature of the offence shapes the discretion: group violence arising from trivial road disputes is treated as a category where the deterrent dimension of criminal process has real weight.</p>

<h2>What remains open</h2>
<p>Dismissal of the application and the accompanying miscellaneous application was a substantive refusal, but it decides only the question of pre-arrest protection. The applicant&rsquo;s defences — on identity, role, common intention under Section 3(5) BNS and the gravity of injuries under Section 110 BNS — remain fully open at the stages of charge and trial, and regular bail after arrest remains a separate remedy judged on its own footing. For those advising accused persons in similar cases, the practical lesson is to engage with investigation notices promptly: non-appearance despite repeated notices figured prominently in the refusal, and cooperation is consistently the strongest single factor in pre-arrest bail outcomes.</p>
